{
  "id": "ep181",
  "duration": 51.83,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.0,
      "text": "A star 12 miles wide physically warped Earth's atmosphere.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "SGR 1806-20 108536main NeutronStar-Print1.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/Goddard Space Flight Center Conceptual Image Lab",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SGR_1806-20_108536main_NeutronStar-Print1.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 4.0,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.0,
      "end": 11.72,
      "text": "The magnetar SGR 1806-20 is a dead star with a magnetic field of 1,000 trillion gauss.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Magnetar Fields.tif",
        "credit": "NASA's Goddard Space Flight Center",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magnetar_Fields.tif"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.0,
          "end": 7.367,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 7.367,
          "end": 11.733,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 11.72,
      "end": 21.215,
      "text": "On December 27, 2004, on the opposite side of the Milky Way, magnetic stress fractured its ultradense core in a massive starquake.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Neutron star magnetic fields simulation.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Jet propulsion laboratory",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neutron_star_magnetic_fields_simulation.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 11.733,
          "end": 15.833,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 15.833,
          "end": 21.2,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 21.215,
      "end": 28.215,
      "text": "In a tenth of a second, the rupture released more energy than the Sun does in 150,000 years.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "SGR 1806-20 108530main cloudballPrint.jpg",
        "credit": "U Harvard",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SGR_1806-20_108530main_cloudballPrint.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 21.2,
          "end": 25.667,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 25.667,
          "end": 28.2,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 28.215,
      "end": 34.81,
      "text": "Traveling 50,000 light-years, the burst momentarily matched the full Moon's brightness in gamma rays.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "2019-01-20 21-58MEZ Vollmond LRGB.jpg",
        "credit": "Rolf-Hempel",
        "license": "CC BY 3.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2019-01-20_21-58MEZ_Vollmond_LRGB.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 28.2,
          "end": 32.267,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 32.267,
          "end": 34.8,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 34.81,
      "end": 41.03,
      "text": "That wave struck our ionosphere, ionizing gas and forcing the upper atmosphere to expand.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Gamma-rays hitting the Earth's atmosphere.tif",
        "credit": "NASA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gamma-rays_hitting_the_Earth%27s_atmosphere.tif"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 34.8,
          "end": 37.033,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 37.033,
          "end": 41.033,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 41.03,
      "end": 46.03,
      "text": "If this erupted within ten light-years, it would have caused mass extinction.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Gamma-rays hitting the Earth's atmosphere.tif",
        "credit": "NASA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gamma-rays_hitting_the_Earth%27s_atmosphere.tif"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 41.033,
          "end": 46.033,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 46.03,
      "end": 51.48,
      "text": "Even from across the galaxy, this tiny starquake still physically warped the world above us.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "SGR 1806-20 108536main NeutronStar-Print1.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/Goddard Space Flight Center Conceptual Image Lab",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SGR_1806-20_108536main_NeutronStar-Print1.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 46.033,
          "end": 51.833,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
